course: dictionary
lesson: 5981
title: Mayonnaise: a thick, creamy sauce
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Mayonnaise is a thick, creamy, pale yellow sauce made from egg yolks, oil and lemon juice or vinegar. People spread it in sandwiches, put it on chips and stir it into salads. Mayo is its short, informal name.
explain: Why does it stay thick and not split into oil and vinegar? Scientists call it an emulsion: tiny drops of oil are mixed into egg and vinegar so they stay spread out. «Egg yolk can keep the droplets apart for good» tells you that the egg is what holds the drops together.
explain: Use context to check a new word. In «a dollop of mayonnaise», a dollop is a blob or spoonful, so mayonnaise is something thick you can scoop. Swap in your meaning: «a blob of thick, creamy sauce» still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Mayonnaise is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some mayonnaise, not two mayonnaises.
fact: Mayo is an informal short form, as in «chips and mayo».
fact: The main ingredients are egg yolks, oil, and lemon juice or vinegar.
fact: An emulsion is a mixture where tiny drops of one liquid are spread through another without separating.
fact: Homemade mayonnaise made with raw eggs should be kept cold.
exercise: Which ingredients are used to make mayonnaise? | Flour, sugar and milk | Butter and salt only | Tomato, cheese and bread | *Egg yolks, oil and lemon juice or vinegar
exercise: Ella says «mayo» is a silly made-up word. What is the right answer? | Mayo is a kind of cheese | Mayo is the Scottish word for wet | *Mayo is an informal short form of mayonnaise | Mayo means a tiny dog
exercise: Which is the sensible way to keep homemade mayonnaise made with raw eggs? | Freeze it in the sun | Keep it warm next to the oven | Leave it on the windowsill all day | *Keep it in the fridge, cold
test: In «a dollop of mayonnaise», what does dollop mean? | A thin liquid that pours easily | A sharp, sour taste | A large empty jar | *A small blob or spoonful
test: What is an emulsion? | A machine that cracks eggs | A sour fruit used in salads | A type of cold pudding | *A mixture where tiny drops of one liquid are spread through another without separating
exam: Why does mayonnaise stay thick and creamy instead of separating into oil and vinegar? | It has so much sugar that it turns thick | *Tiny oil droplets are held apart by the egg yolk | It is frozen before it is served | It is cooked for hours until it sets hard
